WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.000
Phase 2 — Demonstrating the Vulnerable strcpy Operation

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:14.000
Educational demonstration only — intentionally vulnerable.
Run only locally with AddressSanitizer.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Input length and buffer capacity are different quantities.
Phase 1 showed this safely.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
When an input exceeds the logical buffer, extra bytes
may overwrite adjacent data in an unsafe implementation.

5
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:48.000
Phase 1 review: one contiguous array of 20 bytes.

6
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:56.000
Three logical regions: buffer[8] in blue,
criticalFlag in orange, buffer2[8] in green.

7
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:05.000
HELLO is 5 bytes — it fits within the 8-byte logical buffer.

8
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:14.000
ABCDEFGHIJKLMNO is 15 bytes — it exceeds the buffer
by 7 characters. Excess 7 beyond the 8-byte boundary.

9
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.000
In this deliberately constructed model,
remaining bytes appear in adjacent logical regions.

10
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Phase 1 capped the copy with std::min — no actual
out-of-bounds write. It was a safe visualization.

11
00:01:50.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The vulnerable operation: char buffer[8];
strcpy(buffer, input);

12
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:10.000
strcpy has no destination size parameter.
It copies until the first null byte.

13
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:20.000
1234567 is 7 characters plus null — 8 bytes. It fits.
Status: FITS.

14
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:32.000
ABCDEFGHIJKLMNO is 15 characters plus null — 16 bytes
into an 8-byte buffer. 8 bytes past the end.

15
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:40.000
The first 8 bytes fill the buffer: A through H.
Then the boundary ends.

16
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:50.000
The remaining bytes overwrite adjacent stack memory:
I, J, K, L into the next 4 bytes, M, N, O and null beyond.

17
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:08.000
AddressSanitizer detects the invalid write at runtime.

18
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:16.000
ERROR: AddressSanitizer: stack-buffer-overflow

19
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:24.000
WRITE of size 16 at ... thread T0

20
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:33.000
#1 in vulnerableFunction at src/phase2_vulnerable_strcpy.cpp:58
The strcpy call site.

21
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:42.000
This frame has 1 object: [32, 40) 'buffer' line 44
8 bytes at offset 32. Access at offset 40 overflows.

22
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:50.000
SUMMARY: stack-buffer-overflow at src/phase2_vulnerable_strcpy.cpp:58

23
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:58.000
Shadow bytes: 00[f3]f3 — f3 is the stack right redzone,
the guard that makes the overflow visible.

24
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:05.000
==ABORTING==  Program exits with code 1.
No code after strcpy is reliable.

25
00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:28.000
Safety clarification: a real out-of-bounds access
is undefined behavior in C++.

26
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Left: Phase 1 safe model — one array of 20, capped,
no actual OOB.

27
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Right: Phase 2 real vulnerability — unchecked strcpy,
16 bytes into 8, stack-buffer-overflow.

28
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Without sanitizers, the same bug may abort with
stack smashing detected, exit 134,

29
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:02.000
or appear to work silently with -fno-stack-protector,
exit 0. Both are valid undefined behavior outcomes.

30
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Undefined does not mean guaranteed crash.
The compiler owes no requirements after the overflow.

31
00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:58.000
Project progression: Phase 1 visualized the boundary.

32
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:06.000
Phase 2 demonstrated the vulnerable strcpy and
what AddressSanitizer reports.

33
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:14.000
Phase 3 will apply bounds-aware and layered defenses:
safe APIs, canaries, ASLR, NX/DEP, and static analysis.

34
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Takeaway: Input length is not buffer capacity.
Enforce the boundary every time.

35
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:35.000
Phase 2 used only synthetic inputs locally — never
deploy vulnerable code. End of Phase 2.
